ASSISTANT In Rust, the **`Either`** type is not part of the standard library, but it's available in the popular **`either`** crate.
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT ### `Either` Type:
ASSISTANT - Represents a value that can be one of two possible types (`Left` or `Right`).
ASSISTANT - Similar to `Result` but without the semantic meaning of success/error.
ASSISTANT - Useful for working with two distinct types in a unified way.
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT ### From the `either` crate:
ASSISTANT ```rust
ASSISTANT use either::Either;
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT fn process(value: Either<i32, String>) {
ASSISTANT     match value {
ASSISTANT         Either::Left(num) => println!("Number: {}", num),
ASSISTANT         Either::Right(text) => println!("Text: {}", text),
ASSISTANT     }
ASSISTANT }
ASSISTANT ```
ASSISTANT 
ASSISTANT ### In your codebase:
ASSISTANT I noticed in your `nix-crypto-core/src/support.rs` file, you're using:
ASSISTANT ```rust
